1) Research milestones: key breakthroughs, publications, and commercialization progress

The DeepTech Lab develops technology at the precipice of the possible: work that was impossible yesterday, is barely feasible today, and may become pervasive and invisible tomorrow. The lab is committed to solving real people’s real problems using technology rather than staying confined to any one discipline, and to ensuring that solutions move beyond publication toward market readiness. As a result, the lab’s research combines technical advancement with refinement, stakeholder engagement, customer discovery, and commercialization-oriented development.

Breakthrough series 1: data-efficient machine learning
A major focus of the lab is data-efficient machine learning: using lower-quality data from more sources to generate broader, actionable insight at similar or lower cost.
Recent milestones include:
•	advancing a new class of AI-enabled diagnostic systems that can “listen” to machines and detect early signs of failure before breakdown occurs
•	developing an AI Mechanic proof of concept that distinguishes normal from abnormal machine behavior using short audio recordings
•	demonstrating this approach across multiple equipment types, including generators, CNC machines, and compressors
•	showing that these methods can work with low-cost sensing hardware, including smartphones and embedded platforms, creating a practical path toward scalable predictive maintenance
•	maturing the work from isolated models into a more complete end-to-end pipeline connecting data collection, AI-based analysis, and real-time alerts or feedback
•	advancing research in audio AI and perceptual sound quality prediction, including methods that incorporate uncertainty estimation and modern self-supervised audio representations
Key concepts developed in this line of work include:
•	Pareto data: doing more with less data by using suboptimal but widely available sensors to gain broader visibility rather than relying only on ultra-high-fidelity sensing for narrow, expert-level diagnosis
•	shifting from expensive, specialized sensors to lower-cost sensing platforms such as smartphones and Arduino-class microcontrollers
•	smarter methods for signal characterization and normalization across devices
•	new ways to “multiply data,” allowing the lab to capture low-frequency events once and learn from them as though they occur more frequently
•	few-shot and one-shot learning through device-to-device translation
•	device-to-device signal translation, physics-informed signal augmentation, and normalization of signals into a shared representational space
•	methods to improve trust in acoustic characterization models by verifying input audio quality and authenticity through automated mean opinion scoring

Breakthrough series 2: smarter, defensive self-driving
A second major focus is smarter, safer self-driving systems, including autonomy under challenging and high-speed operating conditions.
Recent work includes:
•	developing more data-efficient and resilient approaches to self-driving in difficult scenarios
•	building a robust taxonomy of adversarial events in self-driving
•	developing self-crashing vehicle platforms to help train defensive driving systems safely and systematically
•	creating software to support operation of an autonomous IndyCar
•	exploring game-based learning approaches for how AI systems should interact safely with humans and reflect human morals and ethics, including simulation environments for trolley-problem-like decisions and pedestrian interaction
•	expanding work at the intersection of autonomous systems and audio sensing, identifying an important gap in current vehicle perception stacks: while cameras, radar, and LiDAR are standard, audio remains underused despite carrying important safety-relevant information
These efforts represent a broader research trajectory in AI for physical systems: methods that are technically rigorous, low-cost to deploy, and relevant to real industrial and mobility settings.

